 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
-A generation barrier costs the ratio of the barrier-free throughput to what a generation allows, and that cost can be predicted from the asynchronous run's timing alone; the prediction matched a barrierized twin of our sampler on synthetic runtime laws and on a real tissue simulator. The generation-free sampler removes the barrier and, wherever a simulation costs more than a few milliseconds, converts the simulations it gains into a tighter tolerance. Its estimator targets the smooth-ABC posterior up to a partly measured fidelity ratio, is calibrated in one dimension and conservative in four, and is replayable from the log. What it delivers is bounded by the starting bandwidth and the archive size, and \S\ref{sec:discussion} says how to set them.
+A generation barrier costs the ratio of the barrier-free throughput to the throughput a generation allows, and this cost can be predicted from the asynchronous run's timing alone. The prediction matched a barrierized twin of our sampler on synthetic runtime laws and on a real tissue simulator. The generation-free sampler removes the barrier and, wherever a simulation costs more than a few milliseconds, converts the simulations it gains into a tighter tolerance. Its estimator targets the smooth-ABC posterior up to a partly measured fidelity ratio, is calibrated in one dimension and conservative in four, and is replayable from the log. What it delivers is bounded by the starting bandwidth and the archive size, and \S\ref{sec:discussion} says how to set them.
 
 \subsubsection*{Broader Impact Statement}
 This work is methodology for likelihood-free inference on parallel machines. Its direct effect is on compute: it removes the idle time a generation barrier imposes on heterogeneous simulator workloads, and the prediction of \S\ref{sec:results-barrier} lets a practitioner estimate that saving from their own timing data before spending an allocation. We see no risk specific to the method beyond those of Bayesian inference in general.
